What if payment fails ?
for that we have reconciliation job , which will run after one day or two days , 
which will actually tally this transaction request with the response that is sent by the processor,
And based on that tally , we have to confirm that client that the particular transaction was
successfully done in the backend.

Orchestrator service and Collector Callback Service is connected via event-driven architecture.
And Collector callback service store all the status to kafka broker , and kafka has few
topics -> payment.processor.callback.status , payment.processor.status (T+1)
and orchestrator service will read from kafka.
This is PubSub design pattern.

Now , after day or so , when the final confirmation is done with the bank , the processor
service will again send an acknowledgement of each and every transaction to this collector
payment service and it will send it to another topic called as the payment processor status ,
which have the data of one day delay means it is not the immediate data , but the data which
comes after one day and these status have the final confirmation that whether the
payment was successful or not.

Now , we have to do the final reconciliation.
So, we have reconcile service which will talk to this Kafka broker and will update the database
that we have over Payment Transaction DB into some different tables.
Rather it will have a seperate ledger table which will have final information of
the payment who have done the payment , what is the timestamp on which the payment was done and 
all this information will be there on the last table of this entire ecosystem.


Real life example of CAS , Reconciliation and Idempoteny Key

Suppose you buy a Rs 1,000 T-shirt on amazon and accidentally click Pay twice.

i) CAS (Compare and Swap) - Prevents concurrent requests
   Both request try to change the payment status from NEW to AUTHORIZING.
   Only one succeeds; the other fails because the status has already changed.
   Only the winning request proceeds to initiate authorization.

ii) Authorization - Bank approves the payment (here available bank decreases)
    Amazon sends an authorization request through its payment processor. 
    Your bank checks the transaction and approves Rs1,000 generally reserving the funds.
    Once Amazon receives reliable confirmation , the status changed to AUTHROIZED.

iii) Idempotency - Make Retries safe
     Suppose the bank approves the payment but Amazon deosn't receive the response because of a network timeout.
     Amazon retries using the same idempotency key.
     The payment processor recognizes the same operation and avoids creating a duplicate authorization,
     assuming it correctly supports idempotency.

iv) Reconciliation - finds out what actually happened
    If amazon still doesn't know the payement's outcome , it checks the payment processor's transaction records.
    If authorization succeeded , Amazon updates the status to AUTHORIZED.
    If it failed , Amazon records the failure. If the outcome remains uncertain , it continus recovery
    instead of blindly retrying.

    So, during reconciliation , Amazon accesses
    payment database :- finds unresolved payments :- (for example :-) P101 stuck in AUTHORIZING
    Payment Processor's API :- Checks whether the Rs 1,000 authorization was approved or declined.
    Transaction identifiers :- mathces records using payment ID , transaction ID , amount and currency.

    After reconciliation , if approved -> update status to AUTHORIZED.
                           if declined -> update the payment status accordingly
                           if the outcome remains unknown -> keep it unresolved and continue recovery

v) Capture and settlement - collects and transfer the money.
   After authorization , amazon(merchant) initiaites capture.
   The transaction proceeds through settlement , through which funds move towards the merchant
   according to the payment arrangement.

....settlement happens later.

How to handle scalibility ? 10K Transactions Per Second

We can handle the scaliblity , so one of the method is , database sharding . Here , divide the
Payments database into multiple shards using payment_id as the sharding key.

we are not using merchant_id for sharding because a large merchant like Amazon can generate millions
of payments . If we shard by merchant_id , all its payments may land on the same shart , createing
a bottleneck and creating hot shard.

Since we also need to retrive payment records for a particular merchant_id , we can maintain a
separate database or read-optimized data store indexed by merchant_id to efficiently fetch all
payments associated with that merchant.

When do we use leader-follower async replication ?

Leader :- Handles writes , such as creating payment records and updating payment statuses.
Followers :- Replicate data from the learder asynchronously and can serve read queries.

Use case :- When we have a high volume of payment-status lookups , transaction-history queries , or
            merchant-dashboard requests , followers can handle reads and reduce the leader's workload.
And asyncrhonous replication can cause data loss.
There is a replication lag between when the leader commits a write and when the followers receive it.

so, for merchant-dashboard requests , we can prioritize availability over strong consistency ,
because temporary state data is usually acceptable.

(for example :-)
suppose a customer pays Rs 1,000 for a t-shirt.
the leader database updates the payment status to CAPTURED .
The folloewr hasn't received the update yet because of asynchrnous replication.
the merchant dashboard queries the follower and temporarily sees AUTHORIZED instead of CAPUTURED.

The dashboard can still display the available data rather than failing the request.

Why is this accpetable ?
because of eventual consistency :- the follower eventually recieves the update and displays
                                   CAPTURED.
scalibility :- Multiple followers can distribute read traffic , reducing the leader's workload.

